# Copy to .env and adjust. Sourced by scripts/lib.sh and the Makefile.

# --- Container runtime ---
# Pick ONE. Everything else is derived, so there is nothing to keep in sync.
#   podman | docker | auto   (auto = whichever is installed and running)
#
# Switch with: make use-docker   /   make use-podman
# Changing this needs a cluster rebuild -- kind clusters are not portable
# between runtimes, and the old one becomes invisible rather than erroring.
CONTAINER_CLI=podman

# DERIVED from CONTAINER_CLI by scripts/lib.sh -- do not set these by hand:
#   KIND_EXPERIMENTAL_PROVIDER   podman -> "podman",  docker -> unset
#   HOST_INTERNAL_NAME           podman -> host.containers.internal
#                                docker -> host.docker.internal

# --- Cluster ---
CLUSTER_NAME=llm-lab
KIND_NODE_IMAGE=kindest/node:v1.34.0
REGISTRY_NAME=kind-registry
REGISTRY_PORT=5001

# --- Profile: lite | standard | full ---
# lite     = mock backend + 1 gateway              (~3 GB)
# standard = vllm-cpu x1 + host-bridge + 1 gateway (~7 GB)
# full     = standard + EPP scheduler + Prometheus (~10 GB)
#
# `full` is the default, not `standard`, because the EPP only exists under
# `full` -- and without the EPP, GATEWAY=envoy and GATEWAY=none have nothing to
# route through. Both of those modes fail on `standard`, and the failure looks
# like a gateway bug rather than a missing backend. It fits with VLLM_REPLICAS=1.
PROFILE=full

# --- Model serving ---
# Small enough to actually run on CPU. Do not raise this without raising Docker RAM.
VLLM_MODEL=Qwen/Qwen2.5-0.5B-Instruct
VLLM_SERVED_NAME=qwen-small
# One replica, not two. The node allocates ~9.7Gi and each replica requests 3Gi;
# a second one plus the bridge, mock, EPP and NGF does not fit and simply sits
# Pending. check_capacity() in backends.sh warns about exactly this.
VLLM_REPLICAS=1
VLLM_MAX_MODEL_LEN=2048
# Local mirror tag. `make build-vllm` pulls a prebuilt arm64 image and retags it
# here so cluster rebuilds do not re-download several GB.
VLLM_IMAGE=localhost:5001/vllm-cpu:latest

# Where the prebuilt image comes from. There is NO official vLLM arm64 CPU image;
# upstream publishes CPU wheels for x86_64 only. This community build ships BF16
# for Apple Silicon. Pin a version tag instead of :latest once one works for you.
VLLM_UPSTREAM_IMAGE=docker.io/mekayelanik/vllm-cpu:latest

# Source build is opt-in and fragile on Apple Silicon; see images/vllm-cpu/build.sh
#   METHOD=source make build-vllm
VLLM_REF=v0.11.0

# --- Host inference engine (Metal / real speed) ---
# Ollama:   OLLAMA_HOST=0.0.0.0 ollama serve         -> 11434
# LM Studio: enable "Serve on Local Network"          -> 1234
# mlx_lm:   mlx_lm.server --host 0.0.0.0 --port 8081  -> 8081
HOST_ENGINE_PORT=11434
HOST_ENGINE_MODEL=llama3.2:3b

# --- Ingress tier (always on): NGINX Gateway Fabric ---
# NGF owns north-south Gateway API. The AI gateway sits behind it.
NGF_VERSION=2.6.7
NGF_GATEWAY_CLASS=nginx

# Applies to ALL helm charts (NGF, Envoy Gateway, Envoy AI Gateway).
#   auto   = try the OCI registry, fall back to the chart source in git
#   source = skip OCI entirely. Use this if your helm client cannot do OCI.
CHART_INSTALL_METHOD=auto

# NGF only. Empty = try oci, then source, then plain manifests.
# Pin one to skip the earlier steps:  oci | source | manifest
NGF_INSTALL_METHOD=

# Gateway API channel: standard | experimental
#   standard     -- fine for GATEWAY=envoy|litellm|bifrost
#   experimental -- REQUIRED for GATEWAY=ngf-llmd (InferencePool as an HTTPRoute
#                   backendRef is still an experimental Gateway API feature)
# CRDs are pulled from NGF's version-pinned kustomize so the two cannot skew.
#
# Set to experimental unconditionally. The experimental channel is a strict
# superset of standard, so it costs nothing for the other three gateway modes,
# and it means swapping to GATEWAY=none is a `make gateway` away instead of an
# edit here plus a full `make up` re-run.
GWAPI_CHANNEL=experimental

# --- AI gateway tier: envoy | litellm | bifrost | ngf-llmd ---
# Every overlay publishes the same Service (ai-gateway.llm-gateway:8080), so the
# NGF HTTPRoute is identical across all of them. Swapping is a one-target change.
#   ngf-llmd = NGF IS the inference gateway: it resolves the InferencePool and
#              calls the llm-d Endpoint Picker itself. No AI gateway tier at all.
#              ('none' still works as an alias.)
GATEWAY=envoy

# --- Component versions (pinned; bump deliberately) ---
# Gateway API version is NOT pinned here on purpose -- it comes from NGF's own
# kustomize ref (see GWAPI_CHANNEL), which guarantees a compatible pair.
# Kept in step with whatever GIE version EPP_IMAGE was built against (see below).
# llm-d v0.8.0 vendors sigs.k8s.io/gateway-api-inference-extension v1.5.0, so the
# CRDs are pinned to match; a v1.0.1/v1.5.0 split is exactly the sort of skew that
# shows up later as an unimplemented field rather than a clean error.
GIE_VERSION=v1.5.0
ENVOY_GATEWAY_VERSION=v1.5.6
ENVOY_AI_GATEWAY_VERSION=v1.0.0
# LiteLLM and Bifrost publish no chart repo and no OCI package -- their charts
# exist only in-tree, so these are git refs, not registry versions.
# LiteLLM's admin UI authenticates against Postgres.
#
#   false (default) -- API only. VERIFIED WORKING: smoke passes, /ui loads but
#                      login returns "Authentication Error, Not connected to DB!".
#   true            -- VERIFIED WORKING, but only with headroom. Root cause is
#                      now confirmed by dmesg on the kind node: a real, global
#                      OOM kill (constraint=CONSTRAINT_NONE, not a per-container
#                      limit), reproduced twice, that takes out the migration
#                      Job's python/prisma/node subprocesses together via
#                      memory.oom.group -- not the Postgres startup race the
#                      chart's extraInitContainers hook would fix. On this
#                      9.7Gi node under PROFILE=full, vLLM alone already holds
#                      3-6Gi, leaving too little margin for Postgres + the
#                      migration Job's transient ~1.7Gi spike.
#                      `kubectl scale deployment/vllm-cpu -n llm-serving
#                      --replicas=0`, then `make gateway GW=litellm`, then
#                      scale vllm-cpu back to 1 -- installs clean, migration
#                      Job completes on the first attempt (no retries), and
#                      `/ui/` login returns `login=success` with a proxy_admin
#                      JWT. This is a manual sequencing step, not something
#                      `make gateway` automates, so leave this false for
#                      unattended runs (rebuild, lifecycle, CI-like passes).
LITELLM_WITH_DB=false

LITELLM_CHART_VERSION=1.1.1
LITELLM_CHART_REF=main
BIFROST_CHART_VERSION=2.1.33
BIFROST_CHART_REF=dev

# Images (all must be linux/arm64; `make verify-images` checks this)
# Endpoint Picker. This is llm-d's build, NOT the upstream GIE one, and the
# reason is architecture, not features.
#
# registry.k8s.io/gateway-api-inference-extension/epp publishes amd64 ONLY --
# every tag checked from v0.5.1 through v1.2.0 is a single linux/amd64 manifest
# with no manifest list. It pulls happily on arm64 and then dies inside the Go
# runtime under emulation:
#     runtime: lfstack.push invalid packing ... fatal error: lfstack.push
# which reads like a scheduler bug rather than "wrong CPU architecture".
#
# ghcr.io/llm-d/llm-d-inference-scheduler ships a real linux/arm64 manifest, and
# its cmd/epp/main.go just wraps GIE's own runner.NewRunner(), so the flags below
# are the upstream flags plus llm-d's extra scoring plugins.
#
# `make verify-images` asserts the arm64 manifest; keep it in the list.
EPP_IMAGE=ghcr.io/llm-d/llm-d-inference-scheduler:v0.8.0
NGINX_IMAGE=nginx:1.27-alpine
MOCK_IMAGE=ghcr.io/berriai/litellm:main-stable
